  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Find $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ given\n$$\\log_4 x=\\log_6 y=\\log_9(x+y).$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{y}{x}=\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$.\n\nSet the common logarithm equal to $k$, convert to exponential form and solve the quadratic in $\\frac{x}{y}$.",
      "worked": "Let $k=\\log_4 x=\\log_6 y=\\log_9(x+y)$. Then $x=4^k$, $y=6^k$ and $x+y=9^k$.\n$$\\begin{align*}4^k+6^k&=9^k\\\\\\left(\\frac23\\right)^k+1&=\\left(\\frac32\\right)^k\\\\\\frac{x}{y}+1&=\\frac{y}{x}.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $m=\\dfrac{x}{y}>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}m+1&=\\frac1m\\\\m^2+m&=1\\\\m^2+m-1&=0\\\\m&=\\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $m>0$, reject the negative root.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x}{y}&=\\frac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}\\\\\\frac{y}{x}&=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5-1}\\\\\\frac{y}{x}&=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 105
  },
